.Dd November 15, 2026
.Dt COOK 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm cook
.Nd rebuild the next, seen, and hold merge branches
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl nv
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
rebuilds up to three merge branches in a
.Xr jj 1
repository, in the style of git.git's integration branches.
Topics flow from
.Sy seen
to
.Sy next
to trunk.
.Pp
The description of the
.Sy next
commit lists the topics of every branch, one per line, below a line
that reads
.Li [cook] .
Text above that line is free prose, such as a title and notes.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
next <ref>
seen <ref>
hold <ref>
.Ed
.Pp
A
.Ar ref
is any revision that names a topic: a local bookmark, a remote
bookmark such as
.Li foo@origin ,
a commit id, or a change id.
It is resolved each time
.Nm
runs, so a topic named by a bookmark follows the bookmark as it moves,
and a topic named by a change id follows the change as it is
rewritten.
Name a topic by its commit id to keep merging exactly that commit.
Since a
.Ar ref
is a revset, it must resolve to exactly one commit.
.Pp
A
.Ql #
starts a comment, which runs to the end of the line.
A comment can label a topic named by an id, such as with the bookmark
it came from.
.Pp
.Nm
rebases
.Sy next
onto
.Li trunk()
and each
.Sy next
topic, in the order listed.
It then rebases
.Sy seen
onto
.Sy next
and each
.Sy seen
topic.
If the repository has a
.Sy hold
bookmark,
.Nm
last rebases
.Sy hold
onto
.Li trunk()
and each
.Sy hold
topic, which keeps topics that are set aside apart from
.Sy next
and
.Sy seen .
A branch with no topics sits on its base alone.
After each rebase,
.Nm
removes the branch's redundant parents.
A topic that has landed on trunk is dropped, and so is a base that a
topic already contains.
A topic listed under both
.Sy next
and
.Sy seen
is merged into
.Sy next
only.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl n
Print the
.Xr jj 1
commands instead of running them.
.It Fl v
After rebuilding, try merging each
.Sy seen
topic into
.Sy next
by itself, and print one line for each:
.Li M
and the topic if the merge has no conflicts, or
.Li C
and the topic if it conflicts.
The
.Li M
lines come first, then the
.Li C
lines, each sorted by topic.
A topic that
.Sy next
already contains is skipped.
Promote one clean topic to
.Sy next
and run
.Nm Fl v
again, since a topic that merges cleanly by itself may conflict with
another.
.Nm
removes the trial merges, so they leave nothing behind but entries in
the operation log.
With
.Fl n ,
the topics are tried against
.Sy next
as it stands.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
It fails, and changes nothing, if the description of
.Sy next
has no
.Li [cook]
line, or if a line below it, less any comment, is not blank and is not a
.Li next ,
.Li seen ,
or
.Li hold
line with exactly one
.Ar ref .
It also fails if there is a
.Li hold
line but no
.Sy hold
bookmark, or if a topic does not name exactly one commit.
If any rebase fails,
.Nm
restores the repository to the operation it started from, so that
.Sy next ,
.Sy seen ,
and
.Sy hold
move together or not at all.
.Sh EXAMPLES
A description of
.Sy next
that merges one local topic, one remote topic, and one topic pinned to
a commit, stages a fourth topic in
.Sy seen ,
and holds a fifth in
.Sy hold :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
next

[cook]
next fix-parser
next feature@origin
next 3f1c2a9e # other-feature@origin
seen experiment # needs tests
hold rewrite # waiting on upstream
.Ed
.Pp
Before the first
.Li hold
line, create the
.Sy hold
bookmark on trunk.
Leave the working copy off it, so that its edits stay out of
.Sy hold :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
jj new -m hold 'trunk()'
jj bookmark create hold -r @
jj new 'trunk()'
.Ed
.Pp
Rebuild the branches, and see which
.Sy seen
topics would merge into
.Sy next .
Here
.Li parser-rework
conflicts with
.Sy next :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ cook -v
M docs-typos
M experiment
C parser-rework
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr jj 1
